# Linear automation (ground truth)

**In Progress**, **In Review**, and **Done** are **automation-owned**, not
agent-written. Agents post START/resolution comments and Git/PR artifacts;
Linear's team GitHub automations move issues through those three statuses. See
[Linear GitHub docs — Workflow automation](https://linear.app/docs/github#workflow-automation).

## Cycle

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  SDD["/sdd-to-tdd execution"]
  Start["linear-resolver START comment"]
  Loop["Red Green Refactor"]
  LR["linear-resolver CLOSE-OUT comment"]
  Commit["/commit PASS"]
  Push["/push optional PR arg"]
  PR["draft or open PR"]
  PromoCheck{"base == default branch?"}
  Prep["inject Fixes trailers"]
  OpMerge["Operator merge in GitHub"]
  SDD --> Start
  Start --> Loop
  Loop --> LR
  LR -->|"comment automation"| IR1["In Review optional"]
  LR --> Commit
  Commit -->|"local commit Fixes REAZED-###"| Local["Commit on branch"]
  Local --> Push
  Push -->|"git push + draft/open PR"| IP["In Progress via PR"]
  Push --> PR
  PR --> PromoCheck
  PromoCheck -->|"yes"| Prep
  PromoCheck -->|"no"| Skip["skip prep — trailers aggregate later"]
  Prep -->|"review activity or ready-for-merge"| IR2["In Review via PR"]
  Skip -->|"review activity or ready-for-merge"| IR2
  IR2 --> Ready["PR ready to merge"]
  Ready --> OpMerge
  OpMerge --> Done["Done via PR merge automation"]
```

`/sdd-to-tdd` execution (after plan approval) announces work with a
`linear-resolver` START comment — it does **not** set workflow state. Plan Mode
production does not write Linear. An issue may remain **Todo until a linked PR
exists**. `/commit` stays entirely Linear-free (local commit only, never
pushes). A single `/push` publishes the branch and creates or updates a
**draft** PR; that draft/open PR event is what fires **In Progress**. `/push`
preps the promotion close-out whenever the PR it resolves targets the default
branch, and may **request PR review** (`gh pr edit --add-reviewer`) so
**In Review** can fire from review activity — it never calls `gh pr ready`,
never calls `gh pr merge`, and never calls Linear MCP. `/push` creates PRs as
**drafts** (GitHub Actions jobs are gated on `pull_request.draft == false`).
On a fresh PR **In Review** fires once the **operator** readies the draft
(ready-for-merge) or review activity exists; **In Progress** fires immediately
via the `Draft PR open` automation. The operator is the only actor that merges
a PR; that merge event is what Linear's `On PR merge → Done` automation reacts
to.

## GitHub PR automations

**Configured in Linear Settings → Team → Workflows & automations → Pull request
and commit automations** (per team):

| Event                         | Linear transition                                                       |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Draft PR open                 | → In Progress                                                           |
| PR open                       | → In Progress                                                           |
| PR review request or activity | → In Review                                                             |
| PR ready for merge            | → In Review                                                             |
| **PR merge**                  | **→ Done** (closing-linked PR into **`staging` or the default branch**) |

`On PR merge → Done` is a **team-level automation on the PR-merge event**. It
fires only for a PR that is _closing-linked_ to the issue — a
`Fixes`/`Closes`/`Resolves` + issue ID that Linear can see on **that specific
PR** (title, body, or its own not-previously-merged commits). A merge into
**`staging` or the default branch** both count. Feature PRs base to `staging`
and close at that merge; the default-branch merge is the `staging` promotion
PR. See
[.cursor/rules/staging-accumulator.mdc](.cursor/rules/staging-accumulator.mdc).

## Comment / message automations

The team may also configure **comment or message automations** in Linear
Settings → Team → Workflows & automations (e.g. transition to In Review when a
close-out resolution comment is posted). These are operator-maintained; agents
do not replicate them with `save_issue`.

The structured close-out comment from `linear-resolver` uses stable section
headers (`Root cause:`, `Spec updated:`, `Verification:`, etc.) so team
automations can match them reliably. If the operator changes the trigger
pattern, update the **Maintenance** note below — never reintroduce agent state
writes as a substitute.

The START comment **is** a **plan digest** — a single, bounded summary, never
the plan file itself. Its labels (`Mode:`, `Owning spec:`, `Criteria:`,
`Approval gates:`, `Infra:`, `Full plan:`, `Problem:`, `Approach:`,
`Out-of-scope findings:`) are deliberately disjoint from those close-out
headers. Do **not** add a comment automation that matches the digest or the
`Work started:` line.

## The accumulator-branch gap

This gap applies to **direct commits on `staging`** (the local `/dispatch`
lane), not to feature PRs. Per Linear's GitHub re-merge behavior, a branch that
already merged other PRs does **not** resurface those PRs' links when it is
later merged again. A `Fixes REAZED-###` trailer in a commit that landed
**directly on `staging`** therefore does **not**, by itself, close the issue
when `staging` is later promoted to the default branch — the _promotion PR
itself_ must carry the closing words. `/push` run from `staging` (or pinned
with `/push <promotion-PR-URL>` if auto-discovery would be ambiguous)
aggregates every closing trailer across that promotion PR's commits and
injects them into the PR body — it never merges; the operator merges once
checks are green.

A **feature PR** into `staging` is a different path: its own commits were not
previously merged, so the closing trailer on those commits (and `/commit`'s
`Fixes REAZED-###`) is visible on that PR. `On PR merge → Done` fires at the
staging merge. `/push` does not promotion-prep that PR (base is not the
default branch).

Before a promotion PR exists, GitHub PR automations (`On PR open`, `On PR
review activity`, `On PR ready for merge`) will not fire for _direct_
`staging` commits. The issue may remain **Todo until a linked PR exists**.
START does not move In Progress. The close-out resolution comment (and any
team comment/message automation on it) plus `/push` review-request (on a PR
the operator has readied) provide In Review visibility until the operator
merges.

## Workflow state writers

**No execution-status writes.** Agents never call `save_issue` to set
**In Progress**, **In Review**, or **Done** — in START, CLOSE-OUT, REGISTER
FINDINGS, or GROOM. There is no START carve-out and no PR-open "backup" write:
the draft/open PR event is the In Progress writer.

**START is comment-only.** `linear-resolver` START — delegated as the first
`/sdd-to-tdd` _execution_ action after plan approval — posts a single bounded
`Work started:` summary comment (Problem/Approach/Out-of-scope findings;
bounded to `START_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS` — the plan file itself is never posted to
Linear, in any form). It never calls `save_issue` (not for state, not for
assignee). FEATURE mode runs START only when the plan's `linear_issue` is set;
free-text `bug:` with no tracked issue skips START. The summary runs even
where the issue is already In Review or terminal. START must not call
`save_document`. `save_comment` (and leftover `save_document` if ever used) is
scanned by `linear-spawn-guard`; an oversized `save_comment` is denied by
`linear-comment-size-guard`
([.cursor/hooks/lib/linear-comment-size-policy.mjs](.cursor/hooks/lib/linear-comment-size-policy.mjs)).
It never opens a Cloud Agent spawn door: `save_issue.assignee` (any value,
including `null`), `save_issue.delegate`, or an `@Cursor` mention in a
comment, title, description, document, or `patch` op. Per
[Cursor's Linear docs](https://cursor.com/docs/integrations/linear), those
are what spawn a Cloud Agent. The local `linear-spawn-guard` hook denies them
on `beforeMCPExecution`; that event is unavailable in cloud agents, so a
cloud agent with Linear MCP access is still restrained by this prose. START
MCP failure is visibility-only — the TDD loop continues. Plan Mode production
does not write Linear.

**CLOSE-OUT never writes workflow state.** `linear-resolver` CLOSE-OUT posts
the structured resolution comment only (`save_comment`). Agents and commands
never call `save_issue` to set In Review, In Progress, or Done during fix
close-out.

- **In Progress** — `Draft PR open` / `PR open` when `/push` (or `/intake`)
  publishes a linked PR. Until that PR exists, the issue may remain Todo.
  Never agent-written via `save_issue`.
- **In Review** — GitHub PR review activity when `/push` requests review on
  an already-ready PR, when the **operator** readies `/push`'s draft
  (`gh pr ready` → ready-for-merge), or when the operator reviews directly;
  team comment/message automation on the close-out comment when configured.
  Never agent-written via `save_issue`.
- **Done** — `On PR merge → Done` when a closing-linked PR merges into
  **`staging` or the default branch**. A feature PR's own
  not-previously-merged commits carry `/commit`'s `Fixes REAZED-###` trailer,
  so the staging merge closes the issue. Direct commits on `staging` still
  need the promotion PR to carry the aggregated closing line (`/push`
  injects it when base is the default branch). The **operator** performs that
  merge in the GitHub UI. Never agent-written.

If an In Progress, In Review, or Done automation doesn't fire (mislinked PR,
integration hiccup), that is an **operator fallback in the Linear UI** —
never something an agent compensates for with a direct `save_issue` state
write, and never something delegated to `linear-resolver` as a workaround.
START is not a fallback for a missed PR-open In Progress.

**GROOM mode** (`/triage`) is narrowly scoped, not a general exception: an
operator-approved grooming batch may move an issue **Backlog ↔ Todo**, or to
**Canceled** or **Duplicate** (each with a linking comment), via `save_issue`
— both are terminal, GROOM-settable states, not automation-owned (unlike In
Progress/In Review/Done). A **prunable-class sweep batch** (see
`docs/findings/README.md` Issue-filing policy) may move several issues to
Canceled under one operator confirmation on the named batch instead of
per-issue confirmation — still one linking comment per issue. GROOM must
**never** move **workflow state** of **In Progress, In Review, or Done**.
Those three only ever change via the GitHub PR automations above. GROOM
**may** write **cycle** as a **field-only** update on In Progress / In Review
(current cycle, no state move) when `/triage` names a missing-cycle backfill
— see the cycle policy in `docs/findings/README.md`.

## Issue-property conventions (milestone, cycle, estimate, filing policy)

The **filing floor, attach-over-create ladder, per-run issue cap, ledger TTL,
milestone convention** (M1–M9 names + filing map — never invent
`Launch-blocking`), **cycle policy** (Todo = current cycle; Backlog /
REGISTER FINDINGS unscheduled), and **estimate crosswalk** live in
`docs/findings/README.md` — the single policy `linear-resolver`, `/sdd-to-tdd`
STEP 4C, `/triage`, and `/audit` PART 8 all cite. This file governs
_workflow state_ (In Progress / In Review / Done via GitHub PR automations;
and the GROOM-settable states above);
`docs/findings/README.md` governs _which issues get created at all_ and the
non-workflow properties (priority, milestone, cycle, estimate, labels) set on
them. Update that file, not this one, if the filing policy or property
conventions change.

## Operator merge checklist

The only human-performed step in this cycle is merging in the GitHub UI.
Two paths:

**Feature PR** (head `sdd/REAZED-###` or any non-`staging` feature branch →
base `staging`) — lighter; `/push` does not promotion-prep it:

1. Confirm `/push` opened the PR as `<head> → staging` and requested review
   (or reported the single-operator caveat).
2. Confirm required checks are green (`/push` reports this advisorily; verify
   in the GitHub UI before merging).
3. Merge the feature PR in the GitHub UI. The PR's own `Fixes REAZED-###`
   trailer is what Linear sees; the issue should move to **Done** at this
   merge.
4. If Done does not fire (~1 minute), check the closing link on **that** PR.

**Promotion PR** (head `staging` → default branch) — needed for leftover
**direct commits** on `staging`:

1. Run `/push` from `staging` (or `/push <promotion-PR-URL>` if discovery is
   ambiguous) — confirm the `## Linear close-out` block /
   `Fixes REAZED-###[, REAZED-###, ...]` line is present on **that** PR (not
   just buried in staging commits).
2. Confirm required checks are green (`/push` reports this advisorily; verify
   in the GitHub UI before merging).
3. Merge the promotion PR in the GitHub UI.
4. Verify the linked issue(s) that were only on direct `staging` commits move
   to **Done** in Linear (~1 minute); if not, check the closing link on the
   merged promotion PR itself.

If an In Progress, In Review, or Done automation misfires on either path, fix
the state **in the Linear UI yourself** — never via `linear-resolver`
CLOSE-OUT/GROOM or any agent compensating `save_issue` call. Never `gh pr
merge`.

## Maintenance

If the operator changes the team's Workflows & Automations settings, update
this file:

- The **GitHub PR table** above — `/commit`, `/push`, and `linear-resolver`
  assume `On PR merge → Done` is configured as stated. `On PR open` /
  `Draft PR open` → In Progress is the In Progress writer. `On PR review
request or activity` and `On PR ready for merge` → In Review.
- Any **comment/message automation trigger** the team relies on for In Review
  on close-out (document the pattern here when known; e.g. comment containing
  `Verification:`).
- Do **not** add a Linear automation that moves to In Review or Done from the
  START `Work started:` summary comment — that header and its
  labels (`Mode:`, `Owning spec:`, `Criteria:`, `Approval gates:`, `Infra:`,
  `Full plan:`, `Problem:`, `Approach:`, `Out-of-scope findings:`) are an
  audit trail only. Close-out automations must keep matching close-out
  headers (`Verification:`, `Root cause:`, …), never the START summary.
